#!/bin/sh
# script/card-check, which is milestone 223 (read a card and say whether its kernel and archive match).
#
#   script/card-check                      the payload this tree last built, in target/board
#   script/card-check /Volumes/NIFE        a mounted microSD card, before you walk to the board
#   script/card-check /Volumes/NIFE_STICK  a UEFI stick, EFI/BOOT/*.EFI, from
#                                          milestone 441 (the program that makes the stick)
#   script/card-check target/stick/EFI/BOOT/BOOTRISCV64.EFI     one boot file
#
# A kernel image and the userspace archive beside it are ONE sealed set. The kernel compiles in the
# SHA-256 of every archive entry it may enter and halts at `MEASURED BOOT REFUSED` rather than enter
# bytes that hash to something else. So a card carrying a kernel from one build over an archive from
# another does not fail when it is written; it fails after the walk to the board, the card swap and
# the power cycle. That has happened three times: radon on 2026-08-15, xenon on 2026-09-17, and a
# lane wiring a test on 2026-09-19.
#
# This asks the board's own question on the host. It reads; it never writes, and it never touches a
# device. Point it at a MOUNTED filesystem (/Volumes/NIFE), not at a device node.
#
# Exit codes, because a bench script wants to tell these apart:
#   0  sealed: this pair boots
#   1  not sealed: the board would refuse it, and the output says which half to copy
#   2  nothing to check here (wrong path, or half a payload)
#
# **The kernel's own check is the authority and this is the early warning.** If they ever disagree,
# the board is right and this is the bug (milestone 223's own BUGS section).
#
# BUGS
#   - It cannot say what the kernel expected, only that the archive is not it: a digest the kernel
#     does not carry is not a digest anything can find in the image. Which half is stale is answered
#     instead by comparing both files against target/board, and "one of these two" is the honest
#     answer when this tree has not built a payload.
#   - It says nothing about the boot script or an extlinux.conf, which are the other two ways a card
#     can be wrong; milestone 218 (every boot of the VisionFive 2 needs a human typing four
#     commands into U-Boot) has just changed which of those a card should carry.
#   - Nothing forces anyone to run it on a card written by hand. script/board-image runs it on every
#     payload it builds and on every card it writes, which is as far as a mechanism reaches here.
#
# Name: provisional, minted 2026-09-19 by this milestone's lane. It joins the `*-check` family
# (boot-check, shell-check, qemu-check, cadence-check), which is the family of things that read an
# artifact and return a verdict; deliberately not `board-check`, because the `board-*` family is for
# commands that build a payload or attach to a board and this one does neither. calef has not
# ratified it.

set -eu
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# This boots nothing, and the line still costs nothing: `script/lint` asks every entry point that
# could reach an emulator to resolve the project's QEMU, and an exemption is dearer than one line.
. helpers/qemu-path.sh

exec cargo xtask card-check "$@"
